        "original_id": "2d407d61-ac05-5908-815f-ef2b030f49e6",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: John Erskine, Earl of Mar; George I of Great Britain; James Francis Edward Stuart\n- Events: Jacobite rising of 1715; battle of Sheriffmuir\n- Organizations: House of Stuart\n- Places: Great Britain; Ireland; Scotland; Hanover; Brunswick-Lüneburg; Holy Roman Empire\n\nFacts:\n- From 1698, George I ruled the Duchy and Electorate of Brunswick-Lüneburg, also called Hanover, in the Holy Roman Empire.\n- George I became King of Great Britain and Ireland on 1 August 1714.\n- In 1714, the new king deprived John Erskine, Earl of Mar, of office.\n- The Jacobite rising of 1715, also called the Fifteen or Lord Mar's Revolt, was an attempt by James Francis Edward Stuart to regain the thrones of England, Ireland, and Scotland for the exiled House of Stuart.\n- In 1715, John Erskine, Earl of Mar, raised the standard of rebellion against the Hanoverians.\n- At the battle of Sheriffmuir in November 1715, John Erskine, Earl of Mar's forces outnumbered their opponent but failed to win a victory.\n- George I reigned as King of Great Britain and Ireland until his death on 11 June 1727.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
